# A duty roster, and what it costs to break a rule.
#
# Every other model in this repository states rules that hold absolutely. Real
# rosters are not like that: the cover has to be found, and the question is not
# whether some rule gives way but which one, and at what price.
#
# So the rules here are soft. Each carries the cost of one unit of violation,
# and those costs are the whole objective — there is nothing else to minimise.
# The plan that comes back is the cheapest set of broken promises, and every
# break is reported with its size and its price rather than being absorbed
# silently into an answer that looks clean.
#
#   every_day_is_covered   50   leaving a day with nobody on it
#   honour_days_off        20   putting someone on a day they asked off
#   spread_the_week         6   giving one person a second day
#
# The ordering is the point. A day with no cover is worse than an overridden
# request, which is worse than an uneven week — and because the costs say so,
# the model can be handed an impossible week and still return the plan a
# sensible person would have written.

problem duty_roster "Duty roster"
  "Cover every weekday with one person, honouring requests and spreading the "
  "load where it is possible to do both."

set People = ada, bram, cleo of person
set Days = mon, tue, wed, thu, fri of day

param asked_off[People, Days] default 0 "1 when they asked not to work that day" = {
    (ada, mon): 1, (bram, mon): 1, (cleo, mon): 1 }

param available[People, Days] default 1 "1 when they can work at all that day"

param personal_limit[People] default 2 "Most days anyone may be given in a week"

var on_duty[People, Days] binary means "Put this person on duty this day"

# ------------------------------------------------------------- hard rules

constraint only_when_available "Nobody is put on duty on a day they cannot work"
  category physical
  because "A request can be overridden and an absence cannot, which is the "
          "difference between a rule with a price and a rule without one"
  forall p in People, d in Days where available[p, d] == 0:
    on_duty[p, d] == 0

constraint within_personal_limit "Nobody is given more days than their limit"
  category regulatory
  forall p in People:
    sum(on_duty[p, d] for d in Days) <= personal_limit[p]

# ------------------------------------------------------------- soft rules

constraint every_day_is_covered "Each weekday has exactly one person on duty"
  category operational
  soft penalty 50
  because "A gap in the cover is the worst outcome here, so it is priced above "
          "everything else rather than declared impossible"
  forall d in Days:
    sum(on_duty[p, d] for p in People) == 1

constraint honour_days_off "Nobody works a day they asked off"
  category policy
  soft penalty 20
  because "Requests are kept unless keeping them would leave a day uncovered"
  sum(on_duty[p, d] for p in People, d in Days where asked_off[p, d] == 1) == 0

constraint spread_the_week "Nobody works more than one day in the week"
  category policy
  soft penalty 6
  because "Five days across three people cannot all be single days, so this is "
          "a preference that is expected to be broken — twice — and priced to "
          "be broken last"
  forall p in People:
    sum(on_duty[p, d] for d in Days) <= 1

# ------------------------------------------------------------ assumptions

assume the_costs_are_on_one_scale "The three penalties are comparable amounts"
  because "They are summed, so a gap in the cover being priced at fifty means "
          "it is worth two and a half overridden requests and no less"
  affects every_day_is_covered, honour_days_off, spread_the_week

assume a_rule_is_broken_by_as_little_as_possible "Violation is measured as a "
                                                 "distance, not as a flag"
  because "Charging per unit means a rule missed by one is charged once; "
          "charging per rule would make the second break free"
  affects every_day_is_covered, spread_the_week

# --------------------------------------------------------------- scenarios

scenario short_handed "One person is away all week"
  "Two people cannot cover five days within their limits, so the week cannot be "
  "fully staffed. The plan does not fail: it leaves the cheapest day uncovered, "
  "which is Monday, because covering Monday would break a request as well."
  set available[cleo, mon] to 0
  set available[cleo, tue] to 0
  set available[cleo, wed] to 0
  set available[cleo, thu] to 0
  set available[cleo, fri] to 0
